// Osysharp.Organizations — the groups people belong to: the companies an app's customers work for, and the teams its own
// people are in. Who belongs to which, the roles they hold THERE, and the mail domains that put a newcomer in the right one.
//
// An `Organization` is a base an app derives from — `Company : Organization` for a customer, `Team : Organization` for a
// group of its own staff — or uses as-is. A person's place in one is a `Membership` (with where it came from, so an
// identity provider can keep it in step later), and a role held within one is a `MembershipRole`: a member of the APP's
// own `[Role]` enum, held in that organisation and nowhere else.
//
// ⚑ THE APP SAYS ONE THING — who looks after the organisations:
//     policy ManagesOrganizations => IsAdmin;
//   and states, if it likes, what an organisation's own people may do (`partial entity Membership { security { … } }`).
//
// ⚑ AN ORGANISATION ROLE IS NEVER AN APP ROLE. It is a different table, so no `RoleGrant.Any(…)` policy can ever read a
//   role somebody holds at Acme as a role they hold everywhere — the reason it is not a nullable column on `RoleGrant`.
//
// ⚑ A BASE KIT. The service an organisation you SERVE gets — its tier, its entitlements, its account team and its VIP
//   people — is the add-on `Osysharp.Organizations.Accounts`, which uses this one (D-ORGSPLIT1). An app that only needs
//   "these people belong to this organisation" takes this kit alone.
package Osysharp.Organizations {
  version     "0.4.1";
  minPlatform "0.9.0";
  summary     "customer companies and internal teams: who belongs to which, roles held within one organisation (the app's own roles, scoped), mail domains that put a newcomer with a PROVEN address in the right company, and membership rows that remember their source and external id so single sign-on and SCIM can keep them in step.";
  model "model/**/*.osy";
  use Osysharp.Identity@0;
  tests "tests";
}
